<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Sussex</h1><p class="st-meta">477 CE – 827 CE (approx.) · capital Selsey</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the South Saxons on the English Channel coast, among the earliest Anglo-Saxon realms and the last major one to accept Christianity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelle-of-sussex">Aelle</a> and his three sons landed in 477 and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a> near the shore, later storming the Roman fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pevensey-castle">Anderitum</a> (Pevensey) in 491. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> names Aelle as the first overlord of the southern English. Settlement was hemmed in by the dense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-weald">Weald</a> forest, which isolated the kingdom from its neighbours.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sussex was small and inward-looking, with a farming economy on the coastal plain and the chalk downs. Its kings often stood under Mercian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">West Saxon</a> overlordship. Paganism lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid of York</a> converted the South <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a> in the 680s, founding a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selsey">Selsey</a> on land given by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwealh-of-sussex">Aethelwealh</a>; Selsey later became the seat of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> bishopric. Several kings sometimes ruled at once.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Caedwalla</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Wessex</a> overran Sussex in the 680s, and in the 8th century it fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Ellendun</a> in 825, Sussex submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a> and became a shire. The name survives in the modern counties of East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-sussex.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Sussex" width="320" height="567"><figcaption>Image: Sakurambo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_kingdoms_c_800.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>477 CE – 827 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Selsey</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aelle, Aethelwealh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglo-Saxon paganism; Christianity from the 680s</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sussex, Kingdom of the South Saxons</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=652&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sussex" aria-label="Kingdom of Sussex on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4669 412 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Sussex lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4989 668L4962 619M4989 668L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4962 619h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4989 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4989 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=652&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sussex">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
